/** Process: instance of a program
    Has memory set aside for code and data
    At least 1 thread and own registers/stack
    Can add threads with its own register and stack and can access data in process
    Data space not accessible by other processes, must use pipes and queues to share data

    For a process to run it must acquire a global interpreter lock
        - ensures thread safety
        - prevents simultaneous multi-threading
        - I/O thread performance remains relatively the same

    OS controls the context switching
    During an interrupt, we save the process 1 state into a process control block which holds the following:
        - process state, number, counter
        - registers
        - memory limits
        - list of open files
    Reloads the state of the desired process

    Number of cores = number of same time operations (parallelism)
    Clock speed - number of operations per second (ex. 2ghz)

    Thread: one set of operations assigned to a core, changing order between operations
    Execute some other operations while another thread hangs, shift core's job (concurrent programming)

    
 **/